WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.081 --> 00:00:07.998
When Romeo and Juliet kissed goodbye in the early dawn after their wedding night, they didn't know where or when they might see each other next.

2
00:00:07.998 --> 00:00:16.078
Neither of them spoke of the worry that was deepest in their hearts out of fear that it might come true: they feared that they might never kiss again.

3
00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:22.220
Romeo, with a penalty of death hanging over his head, escaped over the wall and set out for Mantua.

4
00:00:22.221 --> 00:00:28.759
Juliet was still gazing from her balcony long after he had disappeared when her mother and father came into her room.

5
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:34.903
"Are you still weeping for your cousin?" Lady Capulet asked when she saw the tears in her daughter's eyes.

6
00:00:34.904 --> 00:00:37.943
"You cannot wash him from his grave with tears."

7
00:00:37.944 --> 00:00:42.046
"I weep because the man who killed him has fled," said Juliet.

8
00:00:42.047 --> 00:00:45.910
"Yes, that murderer Romeo lives," said her father.

9
00:00:45.911 --> 00:00:52.093
"But take comfort, Juliet, we will have vengeance for Tybalt's death. So weep no more."

10
00:00:52.094 --> 00:00:55.717
"Now we have happier news," began Lady Capulet.

11
00:00:55.718 --> 00:01:01.685
Together, she and Lord Capulet explained the arrangements they had made for Juliet to marry Paris in two days.

12
00:01:01.686 --> 00:01:05.281
"It's impossible! I can't," Juliet gasped.

13
00:01:05.282 --> 00:01:13.172
"Are you too proud to obey us?" her father raged. He had expected her to be grateful for their finding her such a worthy husband.

14
00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:19.608
Juliet dropped to her knees. "Oh Father," she pleaded, "delay the marriage for a month, a week . . ."

15
00:01:19.609 --> 00:01:23.234
"I have given my word to Count Paris; I will not take it back!

16
00:01:23.235 --> 00:01:33.363
On Thursday, you will marry him!" shouted Lord Capulet and, grabbing his wife's hand, he pulled her from the room, leaving Juliet still kneeling on the floor.

17
00:01:33.363 --> 00:01:38.632
At first, Juliet didn't know what to do, but she quickly composed herself.

18
00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:41.339
She put on her cloak and called her nurse.

19
00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:48.231
"Tell my mother I am sorry that I displeased Father and that I have gone to Friar Lawrence for confession," she said.

20
00:01:48.232 --> 00:01:52.587
When she arrived at the friar's cell, he called out to her from the door.

21
00:01:52.588 --> 00:02:00.040
"Ah, Juliet, Count Paris was just here. He asked me to perform your wedding on Thursday! This is terrible!"

22
00:02:00.041 --> 00:02:04.905
Juliet quickly slipped inside his cell and faced him with determination in her eyes.

23
00:02:04.906 --> 00:02:08.802
"God joined my heart to Romeo's when you joined our hands.

24
00:02:08.803 --> 00:02:14.469
I would rather lie dead beside Tybalt in the tomb than force my heart to be a traitor to Romeo."

25
00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:20.389
"Do you truly mean this?" the friar asked, because her words had suggested a desperate plan.

26
00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:28.771
The friar had a potion that would cause whoever drank it to appear cold and lifeless for 42 hours, but then awake as if from a deep slumber.

27
00:02:28.772 --> 00:02:34.746
He gave Juliet a small vial of the potion with instructions to drink it the night before her planned wedding.

28
00:02:34.747 --> 00:02:38.958
He explained that her parents, thinking her dead, would place her in the tomb.

29
00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:45.053
The friar promised to come secretly with Romeo to open the tomb when she awoke and take her to Mantua.

30
00:02:45.054 --> 00:02:49.799
Taking the vial in both hands, Juliet said, "Love will give me courage."

31
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:55.101
As soon as she had gone, the friar hurriedly sat down to write a letter to Romeo.

32
00:02:55.102 --> 00:03:05.139
On Wednesday, Lord Capulet happily hired cooks and extra serving men, while Lady Capulet sent servants all over Verona to buy cakes and flowers for the wedding.

33
00:03:05.148 --> 00:03:12.226
Both were in high spirits because, that morning, Juliet had apologized and quietly agreed to marry Count Paris.

34
00:03:12.227 --> 00:03:18.388
In the evening, Lady Capulet brought Juliet's wedding dress up to her room, along with flowers for her hair.

35
00:03:18.389 --> 00:03:22.309
"Farewell!" Juliet said when her mother had closed the door.

36
00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:29.279
She dressed herself in her wedding robe, wove the flowers into her hair, and retrieved the potion from under her pillow.

37
00:03:29.279 --> 00:03:33.219
She pulled the cap from the vial. For a moment she paused.

38
00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:37.267
"What if the friar is mistaken and I never wake?" she thought.

39
00:03:37.268 --> 00:03:41.633
But putting her fears aside, she raised the vial to her lips.

40
00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:45.527
"Romeo, I come," she said and drank.

41
00:03:45.528 --> 00:03:49.351
The next morning, Juliet's nurse brought breakfast to her room.

42
00:03:49.352 --> 00:03:54.700
"Why, little lamb, you've slept too long. Wake up," she called as she entered.

43
00:03:54.701 --> 00:04:00.613
When she saw Juliet lying on her bed as pale as death, she dropped the breakfast tray and screamed.

44
00:04:00.614 --> 00:04:02.978
The whole household came running.

45
00:04:02.979 --> 00:04:10.382
Her father was the last to reach the bedroom and pushed his way past Lady Capulet and the nurse, who were crying beside the bed.

46
00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:13.704
He shook his daughter, but she could not be roused.

47
00:04:13.704 --> 00:04:18.128
"Alas! My sweet Juliet! She's cold," he cried.

48
00:04:18.129 --> 00:04:24.049
Laying her down, he sobbed, "Death lies on her like a sudden frost on the sweetest flower."

49
00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:31.541
That evening, the family solemnly laid Juliet in the tomb, and the flowers bought for her wedding decorated her funeral instead.

50
00:04:31.542 --> 00:04:38.062
Friar Lawrence had told Romeo to check for messages at the stall of an apothecary who sold drugs and potions.

51
00:04:38.063 --> 00:04:43.267
The day after Juliet's funeral, Romeo went to see if the friar had sent any letters.

52
00:04:43.268 --> 00:04:49.670
News travels fast and bad news fastest, and the story of Juliet's death had already reached Mantua.

53
00:04:49.671 --> 00:04:56.384
So when Romeo asked the apothecary "What news from Verona?" he heard straight away of Juliet's funeral.

54
00:04:56.385 --> 00:04:59.844
His face went pale and his eyes turned wild.

55
00:04:59.845 --> 00:05:06.984
Before he realized what desperate thing he had decided, he demanded from the apothecary his strongest and quickest poison.

56
00:05:07.001 --> 00:05:12.341
The apothecary hesitated, but then pulled a tiny flask from under his counter.

57
00:05:12.342 --> 00:05:18.373
"Even someone with the strength of twenty men would die immediately if he drank this," he said.

58
00:05:18.374 --> 00:05:26.103
Romeo paid for the poison with a small purse of gold, and as he grasped the flask, he felt an emptiness in the pit of his stomach.

59
00:05:26.104 --> 00:05:30.635
"Well, Juliet, I will lie with you tonight," he said.

60
00:05:30.636 --> 00:05:35.794
The family tomb of the Capulets stood in a large churchyard on the outskirts of Verona.

61
00:05:35.795 --> 00:05:40.016
It was dark when Romeo reached it, after riding hard most of the day.

62
00:05:40.018 --> 00:05:46.358
Carrying a small lantern, he approached the tomb, pushed open the stone door, and stepped inside.

63
00:05:46.359 --> 00:05:53.232
He set the lantern down, and his eyes fell on Juliet, lying in her wedding dress on the cold stone.

64
00:05:53.233 --> 00:06:00.823
"Oh my love, my wife!" he said. "Death may have stolen your breath, but it has no power to steal your beauty!"

65
00:06:00.824 --> 00:06:05.446
Her lips looked red, and he thought her cheeks were almost pink with life.

66
00:06:05.447 --> 00:06:11.168
He bent down and kissed her, then with tears clouding his eyes, he drank the poison.

67
00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:18.129
It burned quickly in his veins. Falling, Romeo gasped, "With a kiss, I die!"

68
00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:22.018
Outside the tomb, Friar Lawrence was arriving in a panic.

69
00:06:22.019 --> 00:06:29.295
It was almost the time when Juliet should awake, and he had just learned that the messenger had never reached Mantua with his letter.

70
00:06:29.296 --> 00:06:33.898
He thought he saw a light inside the tomb and hurried across the churchyard.

71
00:06:33.899 --> 00:06:37.803
In the flickering light of the tomb, Juliet's eyes opened.

72
00:06:37.804 --> 00:06:44.995
Her lips parted and her cold figure took a sudden, deep gulp of air. She shivered violently as she revived.

73
00:06:44.996 --> 00:06:50.249
"Where is my Romeo?" she said, expecting to see the friar and Romeo.

74
00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:53.749
Instead, she saw her lover's crumpled body.

75
00:06:53.757 --> 00:06:59.557
The tiny flask was still in his hand. In a flash of despair, she knew what he had done.

76
00:06:59.558 --> 00:07:07.128
"I will kiss your lips; hopefully some poison still lingers there. Oh, your lips are still warm!" she said.

77
00:07:07.129 --> 00:07:17.243
Then she pulled the dagger from Romeo's belt and, without hesitation, stabbed the blade deep into her chest. "Oh dagger, rest there, and let me die!"

78
00:07:17.244 --> 00:07:20.867
Friar Lawrence burst into the tomb as Juliet fell.

79
00:07:20.868 --> 00:07:27.066
The light and the noise in the tomb had alerted the watchman, and he entered the tomb right behind the friar.

80
00:07:27.067 --> 00:07:29.987
"What's this!" he exclaimed.

81
00:07:29.988 --> 00:07:33.884
"Oh pitiful sight!" cried the friar.

82
00:07:33.885 --> 00:07:38.924
"Here is Romeo, dead! And Juliet, dead and bleeding still!

83
00:07:38.925 --> 00:07:42.765
Go, watchman, wake their families. I must tell them all."

84
00:07:42.766 --> 00:07:47.778
Friar Lawrence staggered forward and gazed at the bodies of the two young lovers.

85
00:07:47.779 --> 00:07:54.743
"For never was there a story of more woe, than this of Juliet and her Romeo."

